WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:06.390
JOE RODRIGUEZ: Hello,
and welcome everyone

00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:09.420
to today's session on
Modernizing Texas' Best

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:11.610
Retail Chain with Anthos.

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:14.160
My name is Joe Rodriguez,
Platform Engineering Manager

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:18.990
for H-E-B, responsible for
hybrid cloud deployment.

00:00:18.990 --> 00:00:19.740
JUSTIN TURNER: Hi.

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:20.580
I'm Justin Turner.

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:22.020
I'm the Senior
Engineering Manager

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:23.967
responsible for digital
fulfillment at H-E-B.

00:00:23.967 --> 00:00:25.800
JOE RODRIGUEZ: Today
we'll talk a little bit

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:27.413
about who we are as a company.

00:00:27.413 --> 00:00:29.580
And hopefully peel back the
onion and share with you

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:32.369
our road to platform
modernization with Anthos

00:00:32.369 --> 00:00:34.200
and the effects that
it's having on helping

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:38.250
us ship digital assets to
H-E-B and to our customers.

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:41.100
To be clear, this is not an
exhaustive technical talk,

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:43.290
but rather a
behind-the-scenes look

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:45.120
at some of the thinking
and decision making

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:48.540
that went into building
out the platform

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:50.460
and our technical competency.

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:52.830
Therefore, I will cover
the work and rationale

00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:55.863
that we put into building the
platform, and how we got here.

00:00:55.863 --> 00:00:57.780
And my colleague Justin
will cover the details

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:00.360
of engineering applications
on the platform

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:02.580
for his digital
fulfillment team.

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:03.255
Who are we?

00:01:03.255 --> 00:01:06.040
We are H-E-B. We
are Texas proud.

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:08.400
We believe that no store
does more for Texas

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:11.970
than H-E-B. We are the largest
retail grocer in Texas,

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:14.070
and have the distinct
pleasure of serving

00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:17.100
over 9 million hungry Texans
daily within our store

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:18.680
footprint.

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:20.745
We hail from the great
city of San Antonio.

00:01:20.745 --> 00:01:22.370
But in actuality,
our humble beginnings

00:01:22.370 --> 00:01:25.910
started in Kerrville,
Texas in 1905.

00:01:25.910 --> 00:01:28.670
H-E-B later moved its
headquarters in the 1940s

00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:30.630
to Corpus Christi, Texas.

00:01:30.630 --> 00:01:32.900
But, for the last
35 plus years, we

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:35.820
have called San Antonio home.

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:38.540
Here's an example of one
of our flagship stores

00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:39.950
in the Houston area.

00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:43.290
And although each store is
unique into their own right,

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:45.390
they are all set
up to do the same.

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:48.020
They are set up to efficiently
and effectively serve

00:01:48.020 --> 00:01:50.265
our customers.

00:01:50.265 --> 00:01:52.140
As you can imagine from
a little bit of math,

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:54.210
we are 115 years young.

00:01:54.210 --> 00:01:59.250
In that 115 years, we've managed
to deploy 400 plus stores

00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:00.870
within our fleet.

00:02:00.870 --> 00:02:04.110
Now, those stores range from
central south, east, and now

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:06.600
West Texas.

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:08.220
Over 350 of them.

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:13.207
But now, we also have about 70
plus stores in Northern Mexico.

00:02:13.207 --> 00:02:14.790
According to dunnhumby,
a data science

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:19.230
firm out of the UK
in 2019, they put out

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:22.500
a study to see who is
the best retail grocery

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:25.380
company for the US for 2020.

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:29.500
They're happy to say that
we were voted number one.

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:31.990
Regardless of any accolade,
you should know this--

00:02:31.990 --> 00:02:34.810
as a company, H-E-B
is relentless.

00:02:34.810 --> 00:02:37.420
We are relentlessly
dedicated to our customers.

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:39.820
Getting food into the
homes of hungry families

00:02:39.820 --> 00:02:42.880
in a timely fashion, especially
during this crazy time

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:45.800
in history, is our
number one objective.

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:48.610
Even so, we do this while
trying to provide the best

00:02:48.610 --> 00:02:51.740
service, price,
customer experience,

00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:53.680
whether in our stores or online.

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:55.330
It's who we are.

00:02:55.330 --> 00:02:57.400
Onto platform modernization.

00:02:57.400 --> 00:02:59.410
Why modernize our platform?

00:02:59.410 --> 00:03:02.050
Simply put, it's
about serving Texans.

00:03:02.050 --> 00:03:03.490
It's what we do.

00:03:03.490 --> 00:03:07.360
Getting food into the hands and
onto the tables of our Texans

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:09.310
is what this is about.

00:03:09.310 --> 00:03:12.070
Although it sounds simple,
every day, this task

00:03:12.070 --> 00:03:15.573
seems to get increasingly
more and more complex,

00:03:15.573 --> 00:03:16.990
which is why we
know that if we're

00:03:16.990 --> 00:03:20.410
going to be serving the Lone
Star State over the next 115

00:03:20.410 --> 00:03:23.590
years, this journey to
platform modernization

00:03:23.590 --> 00:03:25.900
and digital
transformation is and will

00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:29.460
be the key to our
future and success.

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:31.250
Now before modernizing
anything, we

00:03:31.250 --> 00:03:33.800
had to do a bit of
introspection and realignment.

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:37.050
In other words, we had
to get our head straight.

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:39.880
Did I mention that
we're 115 years old?

00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:42.235
We should think that
we're a little bit wise.

00:03:42.235 --> 00:03:43.610
Well luckily, we
were wise enough

00:03:43.610 --> 00:03:46.550
to realize that, if we're going
to be solving today's food

00:03:46.550 --> 00:03:49.220
problems with yesterday's
tools and technology,

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:52.350
this would prove out
to be a very tall task.

00:03:52.350 --> 00:03:54.710
So what we did is we
restructured and aligned

00:03:54.710 --> 00:03:57.680
our engineering, operations,
and development teams

00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:00.800
for the future and for success.

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:03.350
The next thing we did was
do a little bit of planning.

00:04:03.350 --> 00:04:06.410
We knew that this digital
transformation does not

00:04:06.410 --> 00:04:08.550
happen overnight.

00:04:08.550 --> 00:04:11.270
So we started to plan for
a minimum of a two to four

00:04:11.270 --> 00:04:12.330
year runway.

00:04:12.330 --> 00:04:14.510
And when it comes to
the platform itself,

00:04:14.510 --> 00:04:16.079
we saw three phases.

00:04:16.079 --> 00:04:17.899
We called it crawl,
walk, and run.

00:04:17.899 --> 00:04:20.990
But I will cover the
first two phases.

00:04:20.990 --> 00:04:23.030
And Justin will make
mention of where

00:04:23.030 --> 00:04:26.863
we're headed in the future at
the end of the presentation.

00:04:26.863 --> 00:04:28.280
One of the first
things we did was

00:04:28.280 --> 00:04:30.560
looked at the state
of our DevOps, right,

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:32.270
and how we should cope.

00:04:32.270 --> 00:04:35.740
H-E-B has this
concept of own brand,

00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:40.090
where we take something
good, like ice cream,

00:04:40.090 --> 00:04:41.650
and make it better.

00:04:41.650 --> 00:04:44.110
DevOps practices and
tooling is no other.

00:04:44.110 --> 00:04:46.720
It is critical to the
path of the future.

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:48.130
Tooling.

00:04:48.130 --> 00:04:51.430
In regards to tooling, this
has to do with newer tools

00:04:51.430 --> 00:04:53.430
like Docker and Kubernetes.

00:04:53.430 --> 00:04:55.840
And when it comes to
delivering applications,

00:04:55.840 --> 00:05:00.870
we really had to take a hard
look at our CI/CD narrative.

00:05:00.870 --> 00:05:04.957
Just by doing this, we
established our early mission,

00:05:04.957 --> 00:05:06.540
which is a bold
statement unto itself.

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:09.300
And that is to empower
developers and accelerate

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:14.710
H-E-B's transformation from
a grocery to a tech company.

00:05:14.710 --> 00:05:17.340
But, like any good
thing, we quickly

00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:19.590
found out that this has
some inverse effects.

00:05:19.590 --> 00:05:23.130
Empowering developers and
acceleration of transformation

00:05:23.130 --> 00:05:25.800
really has some inverse
effects when it comes

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:28.500
to delivering our applications.

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:31.740
Much of what my colleague,
Justin, will cover

00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:35.250
is exactly that-- living in
this new world on this new stack

00:05:35.250 --> 00:05:39.050
and its effects on
application development.

00:05:39.050 --> 00:05:42.160
The next thing we did was
address the issue of change.

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:45.100
Change within the
enterprises is very hard.

00:05:45.100 --> 00:05:46.310
Change is hard in general.

00:05:46.310 --> 00:05:48.887
But for digital transformation,
it is even harder.

00:05:48.887 --> 00:05:50.470
I would care to say
that no one really

00:05:50.470 --> 00:05:52.360
knows just how hard
something is unless you

00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:54.310
have to go through it yourself.

00:05:54.310 --> 00:05:55.840
When it comes to
the enterprise, I

00:05:55.840 --> 00:05:57.550
think that the critical
thing to remember

00:05:57.550 --> 00:05:59.710
is that enterprises
are much like ships.

00:05:59.710 --> 00:06:02.328
They are a little
bit slower to turn.

00:06:02.328 --> 00:06:04.870
Which is distinctly different
from a startup approach, right?

00:06:04.870 --> 00:06:07.600
Startups are like tugboats,
speedboats, or even smaller

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:09.160
vessels.

00:06:09.160 --> 00:06:11.150
They turn on a dime.

00:06:11.150 --> 00:06:13.400
It takes an incredible
amount of retrofit work

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:16.400
to make ships turn on a dime
like these smaller vessels.

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:19.490
From an operational perspective,
one thing we had to do

00:06:19.490 --> 00:06:23.000
was a bit of re learning,
or thinking or learning

00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:25.520
differently, much
of like you would

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:28.400
from going from an
object-oriented programming

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:30.920
language with something
like Java to a functional

00:06:30.920 --> 00:06:34.920
programming language with
something like [INAUDIBLE]..

00:06:34.920 --> 00:06:38.120
The next thing we
did was formulate

00:06:38.120 --> 00:06:42.260
a team that could really help
us move the ball forward.

00:06:42.260 --> 00:06:43.970
What we did was
we formulated what

00:06:43.970 --> 00:06:45.830
we called a GCP Tiger Team.

00:06:45.830 --> 00:06:50.090
This is a shout out to those key
individuals who really helped

00:06:50.090 --> 00:06:52.980
early on to establish
the footprint

00:06:52.980 --> 00:06:55.890
and get us off to the races.

00:06:55.890 --> 00:06:58.700
What they did was really focus
on setting the groundwork,

00:06:58.700 --> 00:06:59.970
right?

00:06:59.970 --> 00:07:04.950
Building that gap between
operations and developers,

00:07:04.950 --> 00:07:07.680
focusing on reshaping
our culture,

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:10.140
introducing those new tools.

00:07:10.140 --> 00:07:13.220
But in addition to that, it's
really taking a hard look

00:07:13.220 --> 00:07:16.320
at changing the computing
mindset of the organization.

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:18.570
Now you may ask, what
do you mean by that?

00:07:18.570 --> 00:07:21.290
Well, this is what we call
our Wizard of Oz moment.

00:07:21.290 --> 00:07:24.555
Well considering that when
we started this, it was 2017.

00:07:24.555 --> 00:07:27.180
And while the rest of the world
was moving towards Troll Factor

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:29.370
App and Cloud
Native, we were still

00:07:29.370 --> 00:07:32.310
living in the tightly coupled
world of servers, VMs,

00:07:32.310 --> 00:07:33.210
and mainframes.

00:07:33.210 --> 00:07:34.260
Think about it.

00:07:34.260 --> 00:07:37.110
Servers, VMs, and mainframes.

00:07:37.110 --> 00:07:38.880
Servers, VMs and
mainframes, oh my!

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:40.200
You get the picture, right?

00:07:40.200 --> 00:07:43.200
What this was about was
rethinking our compute mindset,

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:47.400
decoupling applications from
physical servers or VMs,

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:51.720
and really taking a look at
not treating these resources

00:07:51.720 --> 00:07:54.300
as pets, much like a cat.

00:07:54.300 --> 00:07:56.678
And really treating
them more like cattle.

00:07:56.678 --> 00:07:58.470
I don't know about you,
but in Texas, we're

00:07:58.470 --> 00:08:02.070
way better herding
cattle than we are pets.

00:08:02.070 --> 00:08:05.102
Which led us to our
container first approach.

00:08:05.102 --> 00:08:06.560
We believe that
this is the fastest

00:08:06.560 --> 00:08:08.150
method of reinventing
our platform

00:08:08.150 --> 00:08:11.960
and delivering applications
at scale and into the future.

00:08:11.960 --> 00:08:16.462
We did this by engaging
Google with GCP and GKE.

00:08:16.462 --> 00:08:17.920
One thing that we
didn't want to do

00:08:17.920 --> 00:08:20.330
is we didn't want to
reinvent the wheel.

00:08:20.330 --> 00:08:23.770
So we focused on becoming
consumers and establishing

00:08:23.770 --> 00:08:27.400
that muscle memory that it
takes to get good at delivering

00:08:27.400 --> 00:08:29.340
applications.

00:08:29.340 --> 00:08:31.650
What we focused on and what
we tried to get out of this

00:08:31.650 --> 00:08:34.590
was three key things
for our platform.

00:08:34.590 --> 00:08:37.080
That application decoupling
that we talked about,

00:08:37.080 --> 00:08:40.950
a faster engineering lifecycle,
and a more systematic approach

00:08:40.950 --> 00:08:42.390
to actually
delivering the tools,

00:08:42.390 --> 00:08:44.850
or rather, putting the
continuous in continuous

00:08:44.850 --> 00:08:46.790
integration and delivery.

00:08:49.790 --> 00:08:52.330
For our application
deployment, we

00:08:52.330 --> 00:08:54.490
sought to have more
uniformed observability

00:08:54.490 --> 00:08:56.680
of our applications,
really focus

00:08:56.680 --> 00:09:01.540
on that operational agility
that most high performing

00:09:01.540 --> 00:09:03.940
institutions look
for these days,

00:09:03.940 --> 00:09:09.040
and really taking a policy
driven approach or a security

00:09:09.040 --> 00:09:11.690
as code approach
to applications.

00:09:11.690 --> 00:09:13.420
So wrapping up phase
one, it took over

00:09:13.420 --> 00:09:15.003
about a year and a
half and we managed

00:09:15.003 --> 00:09:19.240
to rewrite and migrate 16 to
18 production mainframe apps

00:09:19.240 --> 00:09:20.310
into GKE.

00:09:20.310 --> 00:09:22.270
And so not to steal
from any of what

00:09:22.270 --> 00:09:23.980
Justin is about to talk about.

00:09:23.980 --> 00:09:28.100
But what we began to notice
was a speed and delivery,

00:09:28.100 --> 00:09:31.510
that operational agility that we
were looking for, consistency,

00:09:31.510 --> 00:09:34.562
and, overall,
developer happiness.

00:09:34.562 --> 00:09:36.270
So now, we're feeling
kind of comfortable

00:09:36.270 --> 00:09:38.680
and walking through with
our heads in the clouds.

00:09:38.680 --> 00:09:40.920
So the question now
became, how do we

00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:42.840
bring this closer to home?

00:09:42.840 --> 00:09:45.540
How do we do this on-prem
for applications that need

00:09:45.540 --> 00:09:47.910
to sit within our four walls?

00:09:47.910 --> 00:09:49.470
Which led to a new
mission, right?

00:09:49.470 --> 00:09:53.360
Was to accelerate our value
creation for our developers.

00:09:53.360 --> 00:09:56.420
And in addition, shift the
unit economics of engineering.

00:09:56.420 --> 00:09:58.890
Which, really, is just an
intelligent way of saying,

00:09:58.890 --> 00:10:04.180
how do we tackle certain
applications to go out first?

00:10:04.180 --> 00:10:07.540
Now, this is where the proper
realignment and introspection

00:10:07.540 --> 00:10:10.060
that I talked about earlier
really, really came into play.

00:10:10.060 --> 00:10:12.010
This meant that in
order to be successful,

00:10:12.010 --> 00:10:14.320
operations,
developers, engineers

00:10:14.320 --> 00:10:15.760
had to be on the same place.

00:10:15.760 --> 00:10:18.880
Such that there was frictionless
development, world class

00:10:18.880 --> 00:10:21.070
operations, and
automatic repeatability

00:10:21.070 --> 00:10:22.280
within our systems.

00:10:22.280 --> 00:10:25.770
Enter Anthos.

00:10:25.770 --> 00:10:27.930
Why Anthos, though, you know?

00:10:27.930 --> 00:10:30.000
There is a variety of
solutions out there

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:33.822
currently in this space.

00:10:33.822 --> 00:10:35.280
But what we really
wanted to do was

00:10:35.280 --> 00:10:38.450
stick really close
to open source.

00:10:38.450 --> 00:10:41.470
Meaning, we really were
looking for a raw Kubernetes

00:10:41.470 --> 00:10:43.160
experience, right?

00:10:43.160 --> 00:10:46.000
Continuing to build on
that technical competency

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:48.010
that we've just got through GKE.

00:10:48.010 --> 00:10:52.150
And again, leverage that
existing support structure

00:10:52.150 --> 00:10:55.970
that Google provides
us, should we need it.

00:10:55.970 --> 00:10:58.460
Again, what this
is about is, why

00:10:58.460 --> 00:11:02.600
we chose Anthos as our platform
for application modernization

00:11:02.600 --> 00:11:06.050
was we were able to leverage
existing knowledge that

00:11:06.050 --> 00:11:07.550
exists within our
data centers, in

00:11:07.550 --> 00:11:09.620
particular with VMware, right?

00:11:09.620 --> 00:11:12.800
Anthos sits on top of VMware,
and we have a very large VMware

00:11:12.800 --> 00:11:16.040
footprint, we were
able to make use

00:11:16.040 --> 00:11:19.460
of our technical
competencies to accelerate

00:11:19.460 --> 00:11:21.910
the delivery of this platform.

00:11:21.910 --> 00:11:25.340
In addition to that, tighter
grained control of deployment

00:11:25.340 --> 00:11:27.740
for compliance applications,
should we need that.

00:11:27.740 --> 00:11:29.540
The proximity to data.

00:11:29.540 --> 00:11:32.840
We cannot put all of our
data out into the cloud.

00:11:32.840 --> 00:11:36.620
So having these workloads run
very closely within our data

00:11:36.620 --> 00:11:41.870
centers puts that data in close
proximity to these new systems.

00:11:41.870 --> 00:11:44.570
Again, leveraging
that existing support

00:11:44.570 --> 00:11:47.630
and leveraging the experience
that we have with Google.

00:11:47.630 --> 00:11:50.290
In addition to
that, what we plan

00:11:50.290 --> 00:11:53.530
to do in the future is use this
as a burstable launch point

00:11:53.530 --> 00:11:55.900
into other clouds, is
the one key feature

00:11:55.900 --> 00:11:59.010
that we haven't made
use of Anthos as of yet.

00:11:59.010 --> 00:12:01.040
So what this gave us were
the essential building

00:12:01.040 --> 00:12:04.220
blocks that we need to move
forward into the future.

00:12:04.220 --> 00:12:07.220
These building blocks were
composed of Kubernetes, service

00:12:07.220 --> 00:12:10.400
mesh technology, and
serverless technology

00:12:10.400 --> 00:12:12.480
that we quite haven't
made use of yet,

00:12:12.480 --> 00:12:15.250
but we will here shortly.

00:12:15.250 --> 00:12:18.370
With that, I'm happy to announce
that we have now officially

00:12:18.370 --> 00:12:22.570
launched our hybrid cloud
solution at H-E-B. It took us

00:12:22.570 --> 00:12:24.190
about nine months
to sit with Google

00:12:24.190 --> 00:12:26.200
and get this thing right.

00:12:26.200 --> 00:12:30.100
But in February of this year,
we kicked off the project.

00:12:30.100 --> 00:12:35.310
Happy to say that now, in
July, we are now in full beta.

00:12:35.310 --> 00:12:37.330
And we hope to go to
full production come

00:12:37.330 --> 00:12:39.340
August, September time frame.

00:12:39.340 --> 00:12:42.280
We'll be sporting a new
application deployment approach

00:12:42.280 --> 00:12:45.280
with get offs and some of
the other tooling like Argo

00:12:45.280 --> 00:12:47.040
and what have you.

00:12:47.040 --> 00:12:48.840
In addition to that,
our initial workloads

00:12:48.840 --> 00:12:52.470
will target only stateless,
non-compliant applications.

00:12:52.470 --> 00:12:55.250
We wholeheartedly
believe that we

00:12:55.250 --> 00:12:58.760
can get an adoption rate of
5% to 15% of the applications

00:12:58.760 --> 00:13:00.070
that we need to re-engineer.

00:13:00.070 --> 00:13:03.180
And we can double that in 2021.

00:13:03.180 --> 00:13:06.660
With that, I'll now turn it
over to my colleague Justin

00:13:06.660 --> 00:13:09.450
to talk about his journey
through modernizing

00:13:09.450 --> 00:13:13.580
digital fulfillment
on our platform.

00:13:13.580 --> 00:13:14.830
JUSTIN TURNER: Hey, everybody.

00:13:14.830 --> 00:13:17.780
For the next part of
this presentation,

00:13:17.780 --> 00:13:20.470
we're going to be going through
a practical walkthrough of how

00:13:20.470 --> 00:13:25.930
we took the path that Joe
blazed with his teams,

00:13:25.930 --> 00:13:29.080
and really utilized that
to modernize and transform

00:13:29.080 --> 00:13:33.120
how we deliver work in
digital fulfillment.

00:13:33.120 --> 00:13:36.030
Digital fulfillment are the
engineering teams and custom

00:13:36.030 --> 00:13:38.490
built tools that power
our curbside home delivery

00:13:38.490 --> 00:13:39.610
business.

00:13:39.610 --> 00:13:42.390
These are made up of web
and mobile applications,

00:13:42.390 --> 00:13:44.580
as well as delivery
logistic smart

00:13:44.580 --> 00:13:47.610
routing services that allow
us to efficiently deliver

00:13:47.610 --> 00:13:50.440
groceries to our customers.

00:13:50.440 --> 00:13:52.830
They are the tools that allow
our make-ready departments

00:13:52.830 --> 00:13:55.290
to on-demand prepare
the items that are

00:13:55.290 --> 00:13:57.770
part of our customer's orders.

00:13:57.770 --> 00:14:00.020
And they are the tools
that allow our partners

00:14:00.020 --> 00:14:03.320
to shop, manage orders,
and hand those orders off

00:14:03.320 --> 00:14:08.245
efficiently and correctly to our
customers and delivery drivers.

00:14:08.245 --> 00:14:10.120
So some of the reasons
we wanted to modernize

00:14:10.120 --> 00:14:12.580
digital fulfillment
were due to the fact

00:14:12.580 --> 00:14:15.340
that each of these
systems grew up

00:14:15.340 --> 00:14:17.830
as a bespoke model that's
serving an independent business

00:14:17.830 --> 00:14:18.420
need.

00:14:18.420 --> 00:14:21.380
And over time, as our
business grew and changed,

00:14:21.380 --> 00:14:24.220
the domains began to
overlap more and more, which

00:14:24.220 --> 00:14:28.210
led to duplicate functionality
in each application.

00:14:28.210 --> 00:14:30.580
As our business grew, we
knew we wanted better tools

00:14:30.580 --> 00:14:33.580
to address the scaling
that came with that growth.

00:14:33.580 --> 00:14:35.523
And due to the nature
of these systems,

00:14:35.523 --> 00:14:36.940
there's a tight
symphony of events

00:14:36.940 --> 00:14:40.510
that need to occur to get
our customers their groceries

00:14:40.510 --> 00:14:41.990
on time and correctly.

00:14:41.990 --> 00:14:46.060
And any disruption can
prevent us from doing that.

00:14:46.060 --> 00:14:48.970
Additionally, the nature of
the business that we serve

00:14:48.970 --> 00:14:52.240
has changed as the
pandemic goes on.

00:14:52.240 --> 00:14:56.290
These tools allow our customers
to get groceries safely.

00:14:56.290 --> 00:14:59.890
And a disruption to that
is something we don't want.

00:14:59.890 --> 00:15:02.150
So uptime is of a
mission critical concern.

00:15:02.150 --> 00:15:04.640
And we wanted better
tooling to address it.

00:15:04.640 --> 00:15:06.310
We also have frequently
changing needs

00:15:06.310 --> 00:15:09.010
due to the nature
of this business.

00:15:09.010 --> 00:15:11.990
Each change that's
requested by the business,

00:15:11.990 --> 00:15:15.400
simple as it may be, may
require complex changes

00:15:15.400 --> 00:15:19.820
across all of these applications
and the entire ecosystem.

00:15:19.820 --> 00:15:24.350
So we knew we wanted to better
serve the need for change.

00:15:24.350 --> 00:15:28.210
So we decided that we needed
a new digital fulfillment

00:15:28.210 --> 00:15:30.400
services ecosystem.

00:15:30.400 --> 00:15:32.920
We asked ourselves, as
an engineering group,

00:15:32.920 --> 00:15:35.620
what we wanted to get out
of a modernization journey

00:15:35.620 --> 00:15:37.140
and rebuilding these systems.

00:15:37.140 --> 00:15:39.040
We knew we wanted to
deliver work faster,

00:15:39.040 --> 00:15:40.990
recover from incidents sooner.

00:15:40.990 --> 00:15:43.840
We wanted to be more
scalable and resilient

00:15:43.840 --> 00:15:46.390
to the realities
and complexities

00:15:46.390 --> 00:15:49.330
of operating a complex system.

00:15:49.330 --> 00:15:52.750
We wanted to empower our
engineers to grow and learn

00:15:52.750 --> 00:15:55.180
more parts of the stack than
they had ever participated

00:15:55.180 --> 00:15:58.030
in before, and learn
a lot of the new cloud

00:15:58.030 --> 00:16:00.350
native tools that are
available to them.

00:16:00.350 --> 00:16:02.050
We also wanted single
sources of truth

00:16:02.050 --> 00:16:04.240
for every domain within
digital fulfillment.

00:16:04.240 --> 00:16:06.250
So we decided that a
microservices approach

00:16:06.250 --> 00:16:08.190
met these needs.

00:16:08.190 --> 00:16:10.945
That said, we knew that
the road to microservices

00:16:10.945 --> 00:16:13.320
was fraught with risks that
we could create a distributed

00:16:13.320 --> 00:16:16.635
monolith, or a situation worse
than what we already had.

00:16:16.635 --> 00:16:18.510
So we came up with a
simple set of principles

00:16:18.510 --> 00:16:23.460
to help us avoid these traps and
be successful in our journey.

00:16:23.460 --> 00:16:25.560
We wanted our services
to be stateless.

00:16:25.560 --> 00:16:27.840
We wanted to reduce code
complexity with everything

00:16:27.840 --> 00:16:28.670
that we did.

00:16:28.670 --> 00:16:31.170
We wanted to encapsulate
functionality within services

00:16:31.170 --> 00:16:33.930
to reduce operational
complexity, as well

00:16:33.930 --> 00:16:36.120
as the chattiness
between services.

00:16:36.120 --> 00:16:38.790
And we wanted loose coupling
between these services

00:16:38.790 --> 00:16:41.790
so that we could fail gracefully
and keep most of the business

00:16:41.790 --> 00:16:43.620
up should a failure occur.

00:16:43.620 --> 00:16:47.120
And we knew we wanted
one database per service

00:16:47.120 --> 00:16:50.490
to enforce the
microservice's discipline,

00:16:50.490 --> 00:16:52.680
and ensure that we didn't
create a situation that

00:16:52.680 --> 00:16:55.530
was bad for ourselves.

00:16:55.530 --> 00:16:57.860
So the next step
was to establish

00:16:57.860 --> 00:16:59.390
our domain driven design.

00:16:59.390 --> 00:17:01.820
And we did this by following
the event storming process.

00:17:01.820 --> 00:17:04.550
This allowed us to define
the events, triggers,

00:17:04.550 --> 00:17:08.119
responses that make up the
digital fulfillment ecosystem.

00:17:08.119 --> 00:17:11.890
From there, we took the
groupings of these events

00:17:11.890 --> 00:17:15.619
and formed our bounded contexts,
which informed our domains

00:17:15.619 --> 00:17:17.540
and ultimately led
to the initial design

00:17:17.540 --> 00:17:20.260
for our services.

00:17:20.260 --> 00:17:23.230
With this domain
driven design, we now

00:17:23.230 --> 00:17:25.849
have what we needed
to get started.

00:17:25.849 --> 00:17:27.910
We knew we didn't want to
show up with everything

00:17:27.910 --> 00:17:29.640
all at once, with a
big bang approach.

00:17:29.640 --> 00:17:32.770
So we picked the overlap between
last mile delivery and curbside

00:17:32.770 --> 00:17:36.370
pickup domains to deliver
our first set of services,

00:17:36.370 --> 00:17:40.240
specifically, in efficiently
loading the delivery vans

00:17:40.240 --> 00:17:42.455
as they arrive to a curbside.

00:17:42.455 --> 00:17:44.830
I'm happy to say that we were
successful in this journey,

00:17:44.830 --> 00:17:47.680
and August of last year, we
delivered our first Cloud

00:17:47.680 --> 00:17:50.670
native services to production.

00:17:50.670 --> 00:17:53.790
It was a painful journey, and
we had a lot of early learnings

00:17:53.790 --> 00:17:55.890
that informed the rest of it.

00:17:55.890 --> 00:17:58.620
We learned, you need to
validate your assumptions early

00:17:58.620 --> 00:18:01.020
and often about your
domain driven design,

00:18:01.020 --> 00:18:03.060
and how you've
designed your services.

00:18:03.060 --> 00:18:06.490
And not be afraid to redraw
boundaries as you see fit.

00:18:06.490 --> 00:18:10.020
But you only learn about it by
validating those assumptions.

00:18:10.020 --> 00:18:12.180
We learned that we
needed to invest time

00:18:12.180 --> 00:18:14.550
in becoming familiar with
Cloud native patterns.

00:18:14.550 --> 00:18:16.710
We had a new tool
set available to us,

00:18:16.710 --> 00:18:18.660
but initially, we only
brought what we already

00:18:18.660 --> 00:18:19.770
knew into the Cloud.

00:18:19.770 --> 00:18:22.800
So we had to invest there.

00:18:22.800 --> 00:18:25.110
You also need to focus
on the discipline

00:18:25.110 --> 00:18:28.470
around the principles
of your services.

00:18:28.470 --> 00:18:30.820
As you get closer
to the finish line,

00:18:30.820 --> 00:18:34.590
the temptation is there to
subvert your principles to meet

00:18:34.590 --> 00:18:36.500
the deadlines or what have you.

00:18:36.500 --> 00:18:38.310
And it might get
you there sooner.

00:18:38.310 --> 00:18:41.010
But it creates a worse situation
for yourself down the road.

00:18:41.010 --> 00:18:42.990
So discipline around
your principles

00:18:42.990 --> 00:18:45.340
is a key and important.

00:18:45.340 --> 00:18:47.460
And we also learned
by doing a little

00:18:47.460 --> 00:18:50.790
and getting those battle scars,
making the mistakes early,

00:18:50.790 --> 00:18:52.150
we learned a lot.

00:18:52.150 --> 00:18:54.390
And it made the rest of
our journey much smoother

00:18:54.390 --> 00:18:57.490
with that experience.

00:18:57.490 --> 00:19:00.160
These changes
allowed us to start

00:19:00.160 --> 00:19:02.050
addressing our culture
in digital fulfillment

00:19:02.050 --> 00:19:04.240
and how we do work.

00:19:04.240 --> 00:19:07.000
We moved from two week
sprints, where we'd package up

00:19:07.000 --> 00:19:09.180
potentially shippable
product increments,

00:19:09.180 --> 00:19:12.880
and the risk associated with
two weeks' worth of work.

00:19:12.880 --> 00:19:16.120
And were able to move to
weekly combine iterations

00:19:16.120 --> 00:19:19.840
where we were continuously
delivering value to production.

00:19:19.840 --> 00:19:24.400
We reformed ourselves from
a giant monolithic team

00:19:24.400 --> 00:19:27.760
into several smaller squads
that could own their domains

00:19:27.760 --> 00:19:29.270
and services end to end.

00:19:29.270 --> 00:19:31.840
And they did that by
becoming full cycle engineers

00:19:31.840 --> 00:19:35.110
and participating in more of the
stack than they had previously.

00:19:35.110 --> 00:19:37.750
We measured our
success in this change

00:19:37.750 --> 00:19:40.900
by using the four key metrics.

00:19:40.900 --> 00:19:43.750
That allowed us to
identify areas of friction

00:19:43.750 --> 00:19:47.020
that prevented us from
becoming high performing teams.

00:19:47.020 --> 00:19:48.640
To become full
cycle engineers, we

00:19:48.640 --> 00:19:51.160
had to build competencies in
parts of the stack we had never

00:19:51.160 --> 00:19:53.890
really operated in before.

00:19:53.890 --> 00:19:56.320
We started with the database
and performing backups

00:19:56.320 --> 00:19:58.090
and restores, as
well as focusing

00:19:58.090 --> 00:20:01.410
on the efficiency of our queries
in a way we never had before.

00:20:01.410 --> 00:20:04.470
We also started building
our own CI/CD pipelines

00:20:04.470 --> 00:20:06.010
with Cloud Build and Spinnaker.

00:20:06.010 --> 00:20:09.180
And this allowed us to do things
like introduce canary analysis

00:20:09.180 --> 00:20:11.070
to our deployments.

00:20:11.070 --> 00:20:13.980
We then built a competency
around Kubernetes

00:20:13.980 --> 00:20:15.660
itself and GKE.

00:20:15.660 --> 00:20:20.040
And that allowed us to operate
our services end to end

00:20:20.040 --> 00:20:24.640
and fully own what
we've built. We then

00:20:24.640 --> 00:20:26.620
learned a lot of
different tools in GCP

00:20:26.620 --> 00:20:30.040
that became available to us,
one of which was BigQuery.

00:20:30.040 --> 00:20:32.410
It allowed us to
build dead dashboards

00:20:32.410 --> 00:20:37.750
on the fly for our business,
as well as understand our data

00:20:37.750 --> 00:20:39.468
in a way we never had before.

00:20:39.468 --> 00:20:41.260
One of the other
competencies that we built

00:20:41.260 --> 00:20:43.480
was feature flagging,
specifically

00:20:43.480 --> 00:20:47.620
utilizing a tool that we built
ourselves for toggling features

00:20:47.620 --> 00:20:51.010
on and off in a way that
makes sense for H-E-B.

00:20:51.010 --> 00:20:53.820
And we open sourced this tool.

00:20:53.820 --> 00:20:56.550
With the help of Joe's
team, we worked with him

00:20:56.550 --> 00:21:00.990
to introduce the role
of SRE to our teams.

00:21:00.990 --> 00:21:02.852
We embedded these
partners on our squads.

00:21:02.852 --> 00:21:04.560
And they focused their
efforts on helping

00:21:04.560 --> 00:21:07.920
us define SLIs, SLOs, and
refining our alerts until they

00:21:07.920 --> 00:21:09.720
were actionable and meaningful.

00:21:09.720 --> 00:21:13.410
They also helped us to find
good error budget policies.

00:21:13.410 --> 00:21:15.960
And our stakeholders
agreed to them,

00:21:15.960 --> 00:21:18.150
which allow us to change
our behavior should

00:21:18.150 --> 00:21:20.410
we exceed our error budget.

00:21:20.410 --> 00:21:23.250
We also refined our
incident response process,

00:21:23.250 --> 00:21:25.230
so that we could put
our engineers on call

00:21:25.230 --> 00:21:27.600
for what they build and
on healthy rotation,

00:21:27.600 --> 00:21:32.570
so they can really demonstrate
that full end-to-end ownership.

00:21:32.570 --> 00:21:34.610
The next thing that we
did was build a practice

00:21:34.610 --> 00:21:35.900
around chaos engineering.

00:21:35.900 --> 00:21:37.700
We did this by
bringing in Gremlin,

00:21:37.700 --> 00:21:40.910
who through the use
of guided game days,

00:21:40.910 --> 00:21:44.360
helped us build a mindset
around introducing failure

00:21:44.360 --> 00:21:46.970
scientifically to what we build.

00:21:46.970 --> 00:21:49.310
This allows us to validate
all of our assumptions

00:21:49.310 --> 00:21:52.550
in our designs and
really understand

00:21:52.550 --> 00:21:55.820
how our system is going to
perform when failures do occur.

00:21:55.820 --> 00:21:58.430
This has grown into red
versus blue exercises, where

00:21:58.430 --> 00:22:00.250
we can practice
incident response,

00:22:00.250 --> 00:22:03.260
and is now growing even further
into us automating our chaos

00:22:03.260 --> 00:22:04.960
engineering.

00:22:04.960 --> 00:22:07.630
So we're at the point where a
new services are in production.

00:22:07.630 --> 00:22:10.090
They're rolling out to stores.

00:22:10.090 --> 00:22:12.580
And by the end of August,
our full digital fulfillment

00:22:12.580 --> 00:22:15.400
ecosystem should be live
in every curbside location

00:22:15.400 --> 00:22:18.800
at H-E-B.

00:22:18.800 --> 00:22:21.590
Some of the key learnings
at this stage of the journey

00:22:21.590 --> 00:22:23.900
have been that change
safety is vital.

00:22:23.900 --> 00:22:27.920
As you start to move faster,
it increases the risk

00:22:27.920 --> 00:22:30.020
that you can push an
error to production.

00:22:30.020 --> 00:22:34.080
And so you need a lot of
automation around your testing,

00:22:34.080 --> 00:22:36.560
so you can move at that
speed, as well as things

00:22:36.560 --> 00:22:39.170
like canary analysis in
your deployment pipeline

00:22:39.170 --> 00:22:41.240
to prevent you from pushing
things to production

00:22:41.240 --> 00:22:43.040
that cause issues.

00:22:43.040 --> 00:22:45.020
You need to size your
services for your teams.

00:22:45.020 --> 00:22:48.110
And what I mean by that is,
as you remove complexity

00:22:48.110 --> 00:22:51.110
from your code bases,
you're introducing it

00:22:51.110 --> 00:22:53.060
into the operation
of your services.

00:22:53.060 --> 00:22:56.120
And not every team needs
thousands of microservices.

00:22:56.120 --> 00:22:58.460
So make sure that how
you size your services

00:22:58.460 --> 00:23:01.417
is something that you
can successfully operate.

00:23:01.417 --> 00:23:03.250
We also learned that
the distributed systems

00:23:03.250 --> 00:23:04.420
are really hard.

00:23:04.420 --> 00:23:06.040
A lot of our issues
here at the end

00:23:06.040 --> 00:23:09.670
have been around fine tuning
our domain driven design

00:23:09.670 --> 00:23:11.230
and redrawing those
boundary lines.

00:23:11.230 --> 00:23:12.950
And you can't be afraid to fail.

00:23:12.950 --> 00:23:15.220
But this is where that
validation of your assumptions

00:23:15.220 --> 00:23:18.490
becomes key, as you
need to refine as you go

00:23:18.490 --> 00:23:21.250
and not have to redesign
your system at the end

00:23:21.250 --> 00:23:24.040
and build the muscle
around distributed systems.

00:23:24.040 --> 00:23:25.640
Ultimately, this doesn't end.

00:23:25.640 --> 00:23:27.090
This is your new normal.

00:23:27.090 --> 00:23:30.190
We're doing this so we can
better serve our business.

00:23:30.190 --> 00:23:32.350
But understanding that
there isn't a finish line

00:23:32.350 --> 00:23:36.110
to all of this is a
key learning for us.

00:23:36.110 --> 00:23:38.210
One of the most important
things we learned

00:23:38.210 --> 00:23:40.250
is that focusing
on the people is

00:23:40.250 --> 00:23:43.580
key to a successful
digital transformation.

00:23:43.580 --> 00:23:46.730
And that's because the
people that do the work

00:23:46.730 --> 00:23:49.457
are the ones that bear
the burden of learning

00:23:49.457 --> 00:23:52.040
all of these new technologies,
changing how they deliver work.

00:23:52.040 --> 00:23:55.440
The technical part of
this is the easiest part.

00:23:55.440 --> 00:23:58.500
Providing a space for your
people to fail safely,

00:23:58.500 --> 00:24:02.550
to learn, and investing in them
is how you navigate this change

00:24:02.550 --> 00:24:05.310
successfully.

00:24:05.310 --> 00:24:07.550
So we're at the next stage.

00:24:07.550 --> 00:24:09.080
At the end of August,
Joe and I will

00:24:09.080 --> 00:24:12.350
be working on our strategy to
take what we've built on GKE

00:24:12.350 --> 00:24:14.870
and transition it
to GKE on-prem.

00:24:14.870 --> 00:24:19.250
And then move that to
the edge in our stores,

00:24:19.250 --> 00:24:21.950
so that we have that ultimate
level of reliability.

00:24:21.950 --> 00:24:24.470
Our services can run right
next to our customers,

00:24:24.470 --> 00:24:26.810
and weather the storm when
there's unexpected events

00:24:26.810 --> 00:24:28.460
like a fiber cut.

00:24:28.460 --> 00:24:29.910
Thank you for
attending our talk.

00:24:29.910 --> 00:24:31.610
